window.METHODS=[
{id:'signs',topic:'1.4',chapter:1,title:'Set signs & reference directions',cue:'A negative answer, node potential, or absorbed power',formula:'Uab = Va − Vb · Pabs = UI',time:'2 min',tags:'negative sign potential power reference ground polarity',why:'Your arrows are assumptions. A negative result tells you the actual direction is opposite; it is useful information.',steps:['Choose ground and label node potentials.','Draw a current arrow and voltage polarity for every unknown.','For a resistor, use U = RI if current enters +; otherwise U = −RI.','Calculate with those references. Interpret the final signs.'],example:'With Va = 12 V and Vb = −10 V, two equal resistors have midpoint Vc = (12 − 10)/2 = 1 V.',trap:'Do not change an arrow halfway through a calculation. A source can absorb power.',check:'Do the algebraic absorbed powers sum to zero?'},
{id:'kcl',topic:'1.8',chapter:1,title:'Solve a branching DC network',cue:'Several unknown branch currents; no easy reduction',formula:'Σ I = 0 · Σ U = 0',time:'5 min',tags:'kcl kvl branch currents nodes loops mesh simultaneous equations',why:'KCL accounts for every ampere at a junction. KVL makes all voltage differences agree around a loop.',steps:['Label nodes and choose branch-current arrows.','Write n − 1 independent KCL equations.','Write b − n + 1 independent KVL equations and substitute U = RI.','Solve the simultaneous equations; keep signs.'],example:'Teacher network: I₁ − I₃ − I₅ = 0; I₅ + I₂ − I₄ = 0. Add three independent loop equations for its five unknown branch currents.',trap:'The third node equation can be redundant. More equations do not automatically mean more information.',check:'Substitute into a node equation and an independent loop equation.'},
{id:'transform',topic:'1.6',chapter:1,title:'Transform & simplify sources',cue:'A source plus resistance makes the network awkward',formula:'Is = Us / R · Us = IsR',time:'4 min',tags:'source transformation current voltage practical series parallel simplify',why:'Two practical sources are interchangeable when their external terminal voltage–current behavior is the same.',steps:['Identify an ideal voltage source with series R, or current source with parallel R.','Keep R unchanged; switch its connection from series to parallel or back.','Point the current-source arrow toward the voltage source’s positive terminal.','Combine the simplified network and restore the desired load quantity.'],example:'10 V with 2 Ω in series → 5 A with 2 Ω in parallel. A 3 Ω load takes 10/(2+3) = 2 A in either model.',trap:'This preserves external behavior, not internal component power. Do not transform an isolated ideal source into a finite practical one.',check:'Does the equivalent preserve the same open-circuit voltage?'},
{id:'superposition',topic:'1.9',chapter:1,title:'Separate multiple sources',cue:'A linear circuit contains several independent sources',formula:'I = I′ + I″ + …',time:'5 min',tags:'superposition multiple sources turn off short open contribution',why:'In a linear network, the signed voltage or current response adds across independent source contributions.',steps:['Keep one independent source active.','Short inactive ideal voltage sources; open inactive ideal current sources. Keep resistors.','Calculate the target using the same reference arrow each time.','Add signed contributions, then calculate power if requested.'],example:'In the teacher’s 4 A / 20 V example, the central current is 2 A + (−1 A) = 1 A downward.',trap:'Never add the separate-source powers. R(I′ + I″)² contains a cross term.',check:'Did you preserve internal resistances and the target reference direction?'},
{id:'thevenin',topic:'1.10',chapter:1,title:'Find the current in one load',cue:'One resistor is the target, especially a bridge load',formula:'IL = Uoc / (Rth + RL)',time:'5 min',tags:'thevenin norton equivalent source one load bridge current terminal resistance',why:'Everything except the load becomes one source and one resistance, making the final load calculation simple.',steps:['Remove only the load and label its two terminals a and b.','Find Uoc = Va − Vb with the original sources active.','Suppress independent sources and find Rth looking into that same port.','Reconnect the load: IL = Uoc/(Rth + RL). For Norton, IN = Uoc/Rth.'],example:'Teacher bridge example: Uoc = 14 V, Rth = 6 Ω, RL = 8 Ω. IL = 14/(6+8) = 1 A from a to b.',trap:'The port can lie between two non-ground nodes. Keep the other bottom connections intact.',check:'Did you remove only the external load, and use the same port for Uoc and Rth?'},
{id:'initial',topic:'2.3',chapter:2,title:'Find the value just after switching',cue:'The question asks for 0⁺ or an initial current/voltage',formula:'uC(0⁺) = uC(0⁻) · iL(0⁺) = iL(0⁻)',time:'4 min',tags:'switching initial 0+ 0- continuity capacitor inductor before after',why:'The stored state survives the switching instant. The rest of the circuit must adapt to that state in its new topology.',steps:['Analyze the pre-switch steady state: C open, L short, if settled DC is given.','Find the actual capacitor voltage and inductor current before switching.','Carry those two state values through 0⁺.','Use the post-switch circuit to calculate other initial quantities.'],example:'Bypassing the teacher’s 2 kΩ feed retains uC = 3 V and iL = 1.5 mA. The capacitor current becomes (6−3)/1k = 3 mA.',trap:'Capacitor current and inductor voltage can jump. Stored values are not automatically zero.',check:'Have you separated the before-switch drawing from the after-switch drawing?'},
{id:'transient',topic:'2.3',chapter:2,title:'Write an RC or RL response',cue:'Find u(t), i(t), settling time, or a threshold time',formula:'f(t) = f∞ + (f0 − f∞)e^(−t/τ)',time:'6 min',tags:'transient rc rl first order response time constant exponential charging discharging',why:'Every stable first-order response moves from its initial value toward a final value at a rate set by one time constant.',steps:['Find f0 from the switching law and the new circuit.','Find f∞ in post-switch DC steady state: C open, L short.','Find resistance seen by the storage element after suppressing independent sources.','Use τ = Rseen C or τ = L/Rseen; substitute all three values into the exponential.'],example:'Embedded RL example: i0 = 2 A, i∞ = 0, Rseen = 2 Ω, L = 1 H. τ = 0.5 s; iL = 2e^(−2t) A and uL = −4e^(−2t) V.',trap:'Use the post-switch circuit for Rseen. A two-state LC circuit does not generally have one exponential.',check:'At t = 0, do you get f0? At infinity, do you get f∞? Is τ in seconds?'},
{id:'phasor',topic:'3.2',chapter:3,title:'Convert & add sine waves',cue:'Same-frequency waveforms with different phases',formula:'√2 I sin(ωt + ψ) ↔ I∠ψ',time:'4 min',tags:'phasor rms peak sine phase amplitude complex polar rectangular add waves',why:'Phasors retain magnitude and relative phase, so a time-varying addition becomes ordinary complex-number addition.',steps:['Use one common frequency and sine reference.','Divide peak amplitudes by √2 to get RMS phasors.','Add in rectangular form; multiply or divide in polar form.','Convert back with the √2 peak factor and the original frequency.'],example:'12.7∠30° + 11∠−60° ≈ 16.8∠−10.9° A. Therefore i ≈ 16.8√2 sin(314t−10.9°) A.',trap:'A phasor alone does not tell you the frequency. Do not add polar magnitudes directly.',check:'Are all phasors RMS values using the same waveform convention?'},
{id:'series',topic:'3.4',chapter:3,title:'Solve a series AC circuit',cue:'The same current flows through R, L, C or impedances',formula:'Z = R + j(XL − XC) · Ĩ = Ũ/Z',time:'4 min',tags:'series ac impedance rlc rl rc resistance reactance angle voltages',why:'Series impedances add as complex numbers. The inductor and capacitor contributions oppose each other.',steps:['Calculate XL = ωL and XC = 1/(ωC).','Add impedances: R, +jXL, −jXC.','Divide the voltage phasor by total Z.','Find element voltages as phasors; classify using the sign of Im(Z).'],example:'10∠0° V across (3+j2) Ω and (2+j3) Ω gives Z = 5+j5 and Ĩ = 1−j1 = √2∠−45° A.',trap:'If UR = UL = UC = 1 V, the total is 1 V, not 3 V. Opposite reactive phasors cancel.',check:'Does ŨR + ŨL + ŨC equal the supply phasor?'},
{id:'parallel',topic:'3.5',chapter:3,title:'Solve parallel AC branches',cue:'Branches share voltage, or the question gives a source current',formula:'Zeq = Z₁Z₂/(Z₁+Z₂)',time:'5 min',tags:'parallel ac current divider branches admittance impedance total current',why:'The common voltage gives each branch its own current and phase. Only their phasor sum flows from the supply.',steps:['Assign the same voltage phasor to every branch.','Find each branch current with Ĩk = Ũ/Zk and add in rectangular form.','Alternatively, combine Zeq and use Ĩ = Ũ/Zeq.','For current division, use the opposite branch’s impedance in the numerator.'],example:'5∠0° A through parallel Z₁ = 2+j2 and Z₂ = 3+j3 gives Ĩ₁ = 3 A and Ĩ₂ = 2 A. Each reactive branch can be nonzero even when their sum cancels.',trap:'Adding branch current magnitudes gives the wrong supply current when phases differ.',check:'Does Ĩ₁ + Ĩ₂ reproduce the source current?'},
{id:'power',topic:'3.6',chapter:3,title:'Calculate AC power',cue:'Find W, var, VA, power factor, or load character',formula:'P = UI cosφ · Q = UI sinφ · S = UI',time:'4 min',tags:'power real active reactive apparent power factor leading lagging watts var va',why:'P measures net energy transfer. Q tracks reactive energy exchange. S measures the RMS voltage-current loading.',steps:['Use RMS voltage and current.','Define φ = voltage phase − current phase.','Calculate P, signed Q, and S = √(P²+Q²).','Add P and Q across branches first; then find total S and PF.'],example:'300 W plus +400 var gives S = 500 VA and PF = 0.6 lagging. Adding −400 var ideally leaves P = 300 W and S = 300 VA.',trap:'A pure inductor or capacitor has P = 0. Capacitive signed Q is negative; apparent-power magnitudes do not generally add.',check:'Are P in W, Q in var, S in VA, and P²+Q² = S²?'},
{id:'units',topic:'1.2',chapter:1,title:'Check units & final answers',cue:'Finish any calculation without losing easy marks',formula:'kΩ × µF = ms · 1 kWh = 3.6×10⁶ J',time:'2 min',tags:'units check energy power kilo micro millisecond seconds joules exam checklist',why:'A correct method can still lose its result through units, signs, or an untested final expression.',steps:['Convert prefixes before substituting, or track a consistent prefix pair.','State the requested quantity, reference direction, units, and phase where applicable.','Check a physical limit, a conservation equation, or the endpoints of a transient.','Keep extra digits during calculation; round at the end.'],example:'6k || 6k || 3k = 1.5 kΩ, not 1.5 Ω. With 10 µF, τ = 1.5k × 10µ = 15 ms.',trap:'The course contains a few copied labels and unit typos. Use the corrected formulas in the reference.',check:'Is the size plausible, and is the result actually the quantity the question asks for?'}
];
window.QUESTIONS=[
{id:'q1',topic:'1.1',chapter:1,type:'choice',prompt:'A microphone → amplifier → loudspeaker system is mainly what type of circuit?',choices:['A signal circuit','A power transmission circuit','An open circuit'],answer:0,explain:'Its primary purpose is transmitting and processing information. Both signal and power circuits consume energy.'},
{id:'q2',topic:'1.2',chapter:1,type:'number',prompt:'A bulb takes 1.6 A at 6 V. Find its power.',value:9.6,unit:'W',explain:'P = UI = 6 × 1.6 = 9.6 W. Power is an energy-transfer rate.'},
{id:'q3',topic:'1.2',chapter:1,type:'number',prompt:'A bulb takes 10 mA at 2 V for 30 minutes. Find its energy use in joules.',value:36,unit:'J',explain:'Convert: I = 0.010 A and t = 1800 s. W = UIt = 2 × 0.010 × 1800 = 36 J.'},
{id:'q4',topic:'1.3',chapter:1,type:'number',prompt:'Us = 125 V, terminal U = 115 V, I = 4 A. Find the source’s internal resistance.',value:2.5,unit:'Ω',explain:'R₀ = (Us − U)/I = (125−115)/4 = 2.5 Ω. Internal loss is 40 W.'},
{id:'q5',topic:'1.3',chapter:1,type:'choice',prompt:'Across an ideal short circuit, which statement must hold?',choices:['Voltage is zero; current depends on the rest of the circuit','Current is zero; voltage can be anything','Both voltage and current must be zero'],answer:0,explain:'An ideal short fixes U = 0, not I = 0. Its current is determined by the connected network.'},
{id:'q6',topic:'1.4',chapter:1,type:'number',prompt:'Two equal series resistors connect nodes at +12 V and −10 V. Find the midpoint potential.',value:1,unit:'V',explain:'Equal resistors divide the 22 V difference equally. Vmid = −10 + 11 = 1 V.'},
{id:'q7',topic:'1.5',chapter:1,type:'choice',prompt:'A source has 3 V positive at its top terminal. 3 A enters that positive terminal. What does it do?',choices:['Absorbs 9 W','Supplies 9 W','Transfers zero power'],answer:0,explain:'The passive convention gives Pabs = UI = +9 W. A device called a source can still absorb power.'},
{id:'q8',topic:'1.6',chapter:1,type:'number',prompt:'Transform a 10 V ideal source with 2 Ω in series into a practical current source. Find Is.',value:5,unit:'A',explain:'Is = Us/R = 10/2 = 5 A. Keep 2 Ω, but connect it in parallel.'},
{id:'q9',topic:'1.6',chapter:1,type:'number',prompt:'40 V + 2 Ω and 20 V + 2 Ω branches feed a parallel 4 Ω load. Find the load current.',value:6,unit:'A',explain:'Transform to 20 A and 10 A in parallel. Rinternal = 2||2 = 1 Ω. Iload = 30 × 1/(1+4) = 6 A.'},
{id:'q10',topic:'1.7',chapter:1,type:'choice',prompt:'A connected network has 3 essential nodes and 5 branches. How many independent KCL and loop equations?',choices:['2 KCL and 3 loop equations','3 KCL and 5 loop equations','2 KCL and 2 loop equations'],answer:0,explain:'n−1 = 2 independent KCL equations; b−n+1 = 3 independent loop equations.'},
{id:'q11',topic:'1.8',chapter:1,type:'number',prompt:'At a node, I₁ = I₃ + I₅. If I₁ = 5 A and I₃ = 2 A, find I₅.',value:3,unit:'A',explain:'KCL: I₅ = 5 − 2 = 3 A in the assigned outgoing direction.'},
{id:'q12',topic:'1.9',chapter:1,type:'choice',prompt:'When finding one source’s superposition contribution, how do you suppress another independent ideal current source?',choices:['Replace it by an open circuit','Replace it by a short circuit','Remove every resistor in its branch'],answer:0,explain:'Set its current to zero: an open circuit. Inactive ideal voltage sources instead become shorts. Preserve resistors.'},
{id:'q13',topic:'1.9',chapter:1,type:'number',prompt:'Two signed source contributions to a branch current are +2 A and −1 A. Find the total.',value:1,unit:'A',explain:'Add algebraically: I = 2 + (−1) = 1 A. Do not add the magnitudes.'},
{id:'q14',topic:'1.10',chapter:1,type:'number',prompt:'For the course’s bridge load, Uth = 14 V and Rth = 6 Ω. Reconnect an 8 Ω load. Find IL.',value:1,unit:'A',explain:'IL = Uth/(Rth+RL) = 14/(6+8) = 1 A. Rth is in series with the load.'},
{id:'q15',topic:'2.1',chapter:2,type:'choice',prompt:'Which two quantities normally remain continuous at an ordinary switching instant?',choices:['Capacitor voltage and inductor current','Capacitor current and inductor voltage','All currents and all voltages'],answer:0,explain:'uC and iL describe the stored state. A capacitor-current or inductor-voltage jump can occur.'},
{id:'q16',topic:'2.2',chapter:2,type:'number',prompt:'The pre-switch inductor current is 1.5 mA. What is its value at 0⁺? Answer in mA.',value:1.5,unit:'mA',explain:'iL(0⁺) = iL(0⁻) = 1.5 mA. The switch can change the voltage and future evolution, not this instantaneous current.'},
{id:'q17',topic:'2.3',chapter:2,type:'number',prompt:'A 6 V node drives a 1 kΩ resistor into a capacitor still at 3 V. Find initial capacitor current in mA.',value:3,unit:'mA',explain:'iC(0⁺) = (6−3)/1000 = 0.003 A = 3 mA, entering the capacitor’s positive terminal.'},
{id:'q18',topic:'2.3',chapter:2,type:'number',prompt:'A capacitor sees 6 kΩ, 6 kΩ, and 3 kΩ all in parallel. Find Rseen in kΩ.',value:1.5,unit:'kΩ',explain:'6k||6k = 3k; 3k||3k = 1.5 kΩ. The slide’s Ω label was a unit typo.'},
{id:'q19',topic:'2.3',chapter:2,type:'number',prompt:'Rseen = 1.5 kΩ and C = 10 µF. Find τ in milliseconds.',value:15,unit:'ms',explain:'τ = RC = 1500 × 10×10⁻⁶ = 0.015 s = 15 ms. kΩ × µF gives ms.'},
{id:'q20',topic:'2.3',chapter:2,type:'choice',prompt:'For a first-order transient, which topology is used to find the time constant?',choices:['The post-switch circuit, with independent sources suppressed','The pre-switch circuit with all sources active','The original drawing without changing anything'],answer:0,explain:'The post-switch network controls the rate of the new response. Suppress independent sources to find the resistance seen by the storage element.'},
{id:'q21',topic:'2.4',chapter:2,type:'number',prompt:'An uncharged capacitor charges toward 10 V. At t = τ, what is its voltage? Answer to 2 decimals.',value:6.32,unit:'V',tol:.03,explain:'uC = 10(1−e⁻¹) ≈ 6.32 V. At one time constant, 63.2% of the change is complete.'},
{id:'q22',topic:'2.4',chapter:2,type:'choice',prompt:'A positively charged capacitor discharges through R. If current is referenced into its positive terminal, its current is…',choices:['Negative','Always positive','Zero for all t'],answer:0,explain:'iC = C duC/dt. The voltage is falling, so duC/dt < 0 and the passive-reference current is negative.'},
{id:'q23',topic:'2.5',chapter:2,type:'number',prompt:'The RL decay example has L = 1 H and Rseen = 2 Ω. Find τ.',value:.5,unit:'s',explain:'τ = L/Rseen = 1/2 = 0.5 s. Therefore iL(t)=2e^(−2t) A for its 2 A initial state.'},
{id:'q24',topic:'2.5',chapter:2,type:'number',prompt:'iL(t) = 2e^(−2t) A through L = 1 H. Find the passive-reference inductor voltage at t = 0⁺.',value:-4,unit:'V',explain:'uL = L diL/dt = 1 × (−4e^(−2t)); at 0⁺, uL = −4 V. The inductor is releasing energy.'},
{id:'q25',topic:'3.1',chapter:3,type:'number',prompt:'A sinusoid has period T = 0.5 s. Find its frequency.',value:2,unit:'Hz',explain:'f = 1/T = 1/0.5 = 2 Hz; ω = 2πf = 4π rad/s.'},
{id:'q26',topic:'3.1',chapter:3,type:'number',prompt:'u = 311 sin(314t − 60°) V. Approximately what is its RMS voltage?',value:219.91,unit:'V',tol:.5,explain:'U = Um/√2 = 311/√2 ≈ 219.91 V, approximately 220 V.'},
{id:'q27',topic:'3.2',chapter:3,type:'choice',prompt:'Ĩ = 20∠30° A is an RMS phasor. Which expression is correct when ω is known?',choices:['i = 20√2 sin(ωt+30°) A','i = 20 sin(1000t+30°) A','i = (20/√2) sin(ωt+30°) A'],answer:0,explain:'RMS to peak requires multiplying by √2. A phasor alone does not specify ω.'},
{id:'q28',topic:'3.2',chapter:3,type:'number',prompt:'Find the angle of (1+j) / (√3/2+j/2). Answer in degrees.',value:15,unit:'°',explain:'Numerator is √2∠45°. Denominator is 1∠30°. Divide magnitudes and subtract angles: √2∠15°.'},
{id:'q29',topic:'3.3',chapter:3,type:'choice',prompt:'In an ideal capacitor under sinusoidal steady state…',choices:['Current leads voltage by 90°','Voltage leads current by 90°','Current and voltage are in phase'],answer:0,explain:'iC = C duC/dt; differentiating a sine advances phase by 90°. The course’s capacitor caption was copied incorrectly.'},
{id:'q30',topic:'3.4',chapter:3,type:'number',prompt:'In a series RLC circuit, UR = UL = UC = 1 V RMS. Find the total RMS voltage.',value:1,unit:'V',explain:'Choose current at 0°. Ũ = 1 + j1 − j1 = 1 V. The reactive voltage phasors cancel.'},
{id:'q31',topic:'3.4',chapter:3,type:'number',prompt:'10∠0° V drives series Z₁ = 3+j2 Ω and Z₂ = 2+j3 Ω. Find the RMS current magnitude.',value:Math.SQRT2,unit:'A',tol:.02,explain:'Z = 5+j5 = 5√2∠45°. Ĩ = 1−j1 = √2∠−45° A, magnitude about 1.414 A.'},
{id:'q32',topic:'3.5',chapter:3,type:'number',prompt:'Ideal parallel R, L, and C branches each carry 5 A RMS. Find the supply RMS current.',value:5,unit:'A',explain:'Ĩ = 5 − j5 + j5 = 5 A. L and C cancel at the supply; their branch currents are not zero.'},
{id:'q33',topic:'3.5',chapter:3,type:'number',prompt:'5 A feeds parallel Z₁ = 2+j2 Ω and Z₂ = 3+j3 Ω. Find the current in Z₁.',value:3,unit:'A',explain:'Ĩ₁ = Z₂/(Z₁+Z₂) × 5 = 3(1+j)/[5(1+j)] × 5 = 3 A.'},
{id:'q34',topic:'3.6',chapter:3,type:'number',prompt:'A combined load has P = 300 W and Q = +400 var. Find its apparent power.',value:500,unit:'VA',explain:'S = √(P²+Q²) = √(300²+400²) = 500 VA. PF = 0.6 lagging.'},
{id:'q35',topic:'3.6',chapter:3,type:'choice',prompt:'Which statement is correct for an ideal inductor in sinusoidal steady state?',choices:['Average real power is zero; reactive power is positive','Average real power equals UI','Both instantaneous and reactive power are always zero'],answer:0,explain:'It stores and returns energy, so average P = 0 while Q = +UI. The copied resistor formulas on one slide were incorrect.'},
{id:'q36',topic:'3.6',chapter:3,type:'choice',prompt:'To combine sinusoidal loads, what should you add first?',choices:['Signed P and signed Q, then compute total S','The apparent-power magnitudes directly','Power-factor values'],answer:0,explain:'Ptotal = ΣP and Qtotal = ΣQ. Stotal = √(Ptotal²+Qtotal²), which generally differs from ΣS.'}
];
window.FORMULAS=[
['DC & signs','Ohm’s law','U = RI','Current enters the terminal marked +. Otherwise U = −RI.','1.5'],['DC & signs','Node voltage','Uab = Va − Vb','Node potentials need a reference. Their difference is reference independent.','1.4'],['DC & signs','Power & energy','P = UI · W = Pt','Use signed passive references for absorbed power. W = Pt assumes constant P.','1.2'],['DC & signs','Practical source','U = Us − IR₀','I is leaving the positive output terminal. Internal loss = I²R₀.','1.3'],['DC & signs','Source transformation','Is = Us/R','Series R becomes parallel R; same R and corresponding polarity.','1.6'],['DC & signs','Thévenin load','IL = Uoc/(Rth+RL)','Remove only the load when finding Uoc and Rth.','1.10'],
['Transients','Switching law','uC(0⁺)=uC(0⁻) · iL(0⁺)=iL(0⁻)','Ordinary finite excitation; not a claim about capacitor current or inductor voltage.','2.2'],['Transients','First-order response','f = f∞ + (f0−f∞)e^(−t/τ)','Stable first-order circuit, fixed post-switch topology and constant excitation.','2.3'],['Transients','Time constant','τRC = RseenC · τRL = L/Rseen','Post-switch resistance seen by the storage element; independent sources suppressed.','2.3'],['Transients','RC charging from zero','uC = Us(1−e^(−t/RC))','Initially uncharged capacitor driven through R by a constant Us.','2.4'],['Transients','RC discharge','uC = U0e^(−t/RC)','Zero-input response. Passive iC = −(U0/R)e^(−t/RC).','2.4'],['Transients','Threshold time','t = −τ ln[(target−f∞)/(f0−f∞)]','Target must lie on the monotonic path; retain the signs in the ratio.','2.4'],
['AC & power','Sine to RMS phasor','√2 I sin(ωt+ψ) ↔ I∠ψ','Use the course’s sine reference and one shared frequency.','3.2'],['AC & power','Reactances','XL = ωL · XC = 1/(ωC)','ω = 2πf. XC is a positive magnitude; ZC = −jXC.','3.3'],['AC & power','Series impedance','Z = R + j(XL−XC)','Add complex impedances; scalar magnitude relation is U = |Z|I.','3.4'],['AC & power','Parallel impedance','Zeq = Z₁Z₂/(Z₁+Z₂)','Use complex values. For more branches, add 1/Z.','3.5'],['AC & power','Current division','Ĩ₁ = Z₂Ĩ/(Z₁+Z₂)','Opposite branch impedance in the numerator.','3.5'],['AC & power','AC power','P=UI cosφ · Q=UI sinφ · S=UI','RMS values. φ = ψu−ψi. Capacitive signed Q is negative.','3.6'],['AC & power','Power triangle','S = √(P²+Q²) · PF = P/S','Add P and Q first. State leading or lagging with the power factor.','3.6']
];
window.FORMULAS.push(
['DC & signs','Kirchhoff’s laws','ΣI = 0 at a node · ΣU = 0 around a loop','Use algebraic signs from one consistent set of current arrows and loop traversal directions.','1.7'],
['DC & signs','Resistor combinations','Rseries = ΣR · 1/Rparallel = Σ(1/R)','Series elements carry the same current; parallel elements connect across the same two nodes.','1.5'],
['DC & signs','Voltage division','Uk = Us Rk/(ΣR)','An unloaded series resistor chain; loading changes the equivalent resistance.','1.5'],
['DC & signs','Two-branch DC current division','I₁ = I R₂/(R₁+R₂)','Parallel resistor branches. Use the other branch resistance in the numerator.','1.8'],
['DC & signs','Superposition','Response = algebraic sum of source contributions','Linear circuit only. Short independent ideal voltage sources and open independent ideal current sources; power is not superposed.','1.9'],
['DC & signs','Energy storage','WC = ½CuC² · WL = ½LiL²','Ideal capacitor or inductor. Energy is in joules when C is in farads and L in henries.','1.5'],
['Transients','Storage-element laws','iC = C duC/dt · uL = L diL/dt','Passive references: current enters the terminal marked positive.','2.2'],
['Transients','RL current response','iL = i∞ + (i0−i∞)e^(−t/τ) · τ = L/Rseen','Stable first-order RL network; use post-switch resistance and continuity of iL at the switching instant.','2.5'],
['Transients','Settling landmarks','1τ: 63.2% · 3τ: 95.0% · 5τ: 99.3%','Fraction of the total change completed by a stable first-order response; exact steady state is approached asymptotically.','2.3'],
['AC & power','Frequency, period and peak','f = 1/T · ω = 2πf · Um = √2 U','The peak-to-RMS relation applies to a pure sinusoid. Angles in ωt are in radians.','3.1'],
['AC & power','Element impedances','ZR = R · ZL = jωL · ZC = 1/(jωC)','Sinusoidal steady state at a single frequency. Capacitor impedance has negative imaginary part.','3.3'],
['AC & power','Phasor arithmetic','Add in rectangular form; multiply/divide in polar form','Convert a+jb to magnitude √(a²+b²) and a quadrant-correct angle. Do not add magnitudes of phase-shifted quantities.','3.2']
);
